>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Standards · Lithium-Ion · Last reviewed 2026-09-22</p>
      <h1>IEC 62660 vs IEC 62133: EV Cells vs Portable Cells</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Two IEC lithium-ion standards, two different worlds. <strong>IEC 62660</strong> is written for the cells that
        propel electric vehicles — performance, life and abuse at cell level. <strong>IEC 62133</strong> is the safety
        standard for portable cells — the ones in phones, power tools and e-bikes. A certificate from one says nothing
        about the other.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> <strong>IEC 62660</strong> = lithium-ion <em>cell</em> standard for
        <strong>EV propulsion</strong> — capacity, power, cycle life, calendar life plus cell-level abuse tests.
        <strong>IEC 62133</strong> = <em>safety</em> standard for <strong>portable sealed secondary lithium cells and
        batteries</strong> — overcharge, short circuit, forced discharge, thermal abuse. If a datasheet shows "IEC 62133
        passed" for a cell intended for a vehicle pack, the qualification does not apply.
      </div>

      <h2>Side-by-side comparison</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>IEC 62660</th><th>IEC 62133</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Scope</strong></td><td>Lithium-ion cells for EV propulsion</td><td>Portable sealed secondary lithium cells/batteries</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Primary focus</strong></td><td>Performance and life</td><td>Safety</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Typical tests</strong></td><td>Capacity, energy, power, cycle life, calendar life, cell-level abuse</td><td>Overcharge, short circuit, forced discharge, thermal abuse, drop, mechanical</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Where it appears</strong></td><td>EV cell datasheets, propulsion R&D</td><td>Phones, laptops, power tools, e-bikes, battery packs</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Pack-level coverage</strong></td><td>No — cell level; packs use UN 38.3, UL 2580, SAE J2464, GB 38031</td><td>Cells and small portable packs</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">
        Scopes per the standards' published summaries on the World Battery Hub testing hub
        (<a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/iec-62660/">IEC 62660</a> ·
        <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/iec-62133/">IEC 62133</a>).
      </p>

      <h2>What IEC 62660 actually covers</h2>
      <p>
        IEC 62660 is structured as the <strong>performance and reliability</strong> standard for EV propulsion cells.
        Its core measurements are capacity and energy at specified rates, power capability (what the cell can deliver
        in acceleration bursts), <strong>cycle life</strong> (capacity retention over repeated charge/discharge) and
        <strong>calendar life</strong> (storage aging at temperature). It also includes cell-level abuse tests —
        overcharge, short circuit, thermal exposure — as a first filter before pack-level work begins.
      </p>
      <p>
        What it does <em>not</em> do is certify a vehicle battery system. Pack-level safety and abuse for EVs sits in
        separate documents: <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/un-38-3/">UN 38.3</a> for transport,
        <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/ul-2580/">UL 2580</a> in North America,
        <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/sae-j2464/">SAE J2464</a> for abuse testing and GB 38031
        in China. The testing hub's <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/abuse-testing/">abuse testing</a> page
        maps that landscape.
      </p>

      <h2>What IEC 62133 actually covers</h2>
      <p>
        IEC 62133 is a <strong>safety</strong> standard: it defines the abuse a portable cell must survive without
        fire, explosion or leakage — overcharge beyond the normal limit, external short circuit, forced discharge,
        thermal abuse at elevated temperature, plus mechanical tests (drop, crush, impact) on the portable products'
        cells and packs. It is the reference most consumer-device and power-tool suppliers cite, and it is often
        demanded by importers as a precondition for shipping portable packs.
      </p>
      <p>
        The confusion arises because e-bikes and light EVs sit in the middle: their cells are tested to 62133 for the
        portable-safety requirements, while the propulsion qualification follows the 62660-style path. A cell can pass
        both — but <strong>passing 62133 proves safety, not EV-grade performance or life</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>The buyer's rule: match the standard to the application</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>EV, PHEV, heavy mobility pack</strong> — the cell qualification is IEC 62660 (plus regional pack standards); a 62133-only datasheet is a red flag.</li>
        <li><strong>Portable devices, power tools, e-bike packs</strong> — IEC 62133 is the applicable safety reference.</li>
        <li><strong>Energy storage systems</strong> — neither standard is the primary gate; stationary storage follows its own chain (UL 1973, IEC 62619 and related), so check which standard the datasheet claims before trusting it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The general discipline is the same as with lead-acid CCA: <strong>a number or certificate is only meaningful
        under its named standard</strong> — see <a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">the CCA parallel</a> and the
        <a href="/standards/">standards hub</a> for the full directory.
      </p>

      <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>IEC 62660 = <strong>EV propulsion cells</strong> (performance, cycle life, calendar life, cell abuse).</li>
          <li>IEC 62133 = <strong>portable cell safety</strong> (overcharge, short circuit, thermal abuse).</li>
          <li>A 62133 pass <strong>does not qualify a cell for EV propulsion</strong> — and neither standard certifies a vehicle pack.</li>
          <li>Match the standard to the application before trusting a certificate on a datasheet.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>What procurement buyers should check</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Does the supplier's datasheet cite the standard that matches the application — 62660 for EV cells, 62133 for portable — or the wrong one to look qualified?</li>
        <li>For EV-cell sourcing: is the 62660 test report (capacity, cycle life, calendar life, abuse) available, not just a summary line?</li>
        <li>For portable packs: is the 62133 certificate current, from an accredited lab, and does it cover the exact cell model being shipped?</li>
        <li>Is transport certification (UN 38.3) present separately — a 62133 pass does not cover transport requirements?</li>
        <li>Does the <a href="/procurement/how-to-read-battery-datasheet/">datasheet reading</a> distinguish standard scope from marketing claims, as with the lead-acid CCA trap in <a href="/procurement/verify-cca-claims-bulk-purchase/">CCA verification</a>?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What is the difference between IEC 62660 and IEC 62133?</h3>
      <p>Application and focus. IEC 62660 covers lithium-ion cells for electric vehicle propulsion — performance, cycle life and abuse testing at cell level. IEC 62133 covers safety requirements for portable sealed secondary lithium cells and batteries — phones, tools, e-bikes — with tests for overcharge, short circuit and thermal abuse. A cell can be tested to both, but a 62133 certificate is not an EV qualification.</p>
      <h3>Does IEC 62133 apply to EV batteries?</h3>
      <p>No. IEC 62133 scopes portable applications. EV propulsion cells fall under IEC 62660 (cell level), with pack-level safety covered by standards such as UN 38.3 for transport and regional standards like SAE J2464, UL 2580 or GB 38031 for vehicle systems.</p>
      <h3>Is IEC 62660 a safety standard?</h3>
      <p>Partly. IEC 62660 is primarily a performance and life standard — capacity, energy, power, cycle life and calendar life — but it also includes abuse tests (overcharge, short circuit, thermal) at cell level. Pack-level vehicle safety is handled by separate standards, not by 62660 alone.</p>
      <h3>Which standard covers energy storage system batteries?</h3>
      <p>Neither primarily. Stationary storage follows its own chain — IEC 62619 for industrial cells, UL 1973 in North America and related regional documents. Check which standard the datasheet actually claims before relying on a 62133 or 62660 certificate in a storage context.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Cross-verified from the standards' published scopes and the testing references on the World Battery Hub testing
        hub; where sources differ on fine detail, the difference is flagged rather than silently resolved.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>IEC 62660 — lithium-ion cell standard for EV propulsion; performance, cycle life and cell-level abuse.</li>
        <li>IEC 62133 — safety requirements for portable sealed secondary lithium cells and batteries.</li>
        <li><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/iec-62660/">Testing hub — IEC 62660 reference page</a> and <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/iec-62133/">IEC 62133 reference page</a>.</li>
        <li>UN 38.3, UL 2580, SAE J2464, GB 38031 scopes — for the pack-level standards referenced in context.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-22</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
